= Formal language — Simple =

A '''formal language''' is a precisely defined set of strings made from a set of symbols.

For example, if the symbols are 0 and 1, a formal language might contain exactly the binary strings that end in 01.

A grammar can generate a language.

An automaton can recognize a language.

The language itself is the set of valid strings, not the grammar or machine.

Formal languages are used in logic, programming languages, compilers, and theoretical computer science.

